DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The ssppuu__ccrreeaattee() system call is used on PowerPC machines that implement
       the  Cell  Broadband Engine Architecture in order to access Synergistic
       Processor Units (SPUs).  It creates a new logical context for an SPU in
       _p_a_t_h and returns a file descriptor associated with it.  _p_a_t_h must refer
       to a nonexistent directory in the mount point  of  the  SPU  filesystem
       (ssppuuffss).  If ssppuu__ccrreeaattee() is successful, a directory is created at _p_a_t_h
       and it is populated with the files described in ssppuuffss(7).

       When  a  context  is  created, the returned file descriptor can only be
       passed to ssppuu__rruunn(2), used as the _d_i_r_f_d argument to the **aatt  family  of
       system calls (e.g., ooppeennaatt(2)), or closed; other operations are not de‐
       fined.   A  logical SPU context is destroyed (along with all files cre‐
       ated within the context’s _p_a_t_h directory) once the  last  reference  to
       the  context has gone; this usually occurs when the file descriptor re‐
       turned by ssppuu__ccrreeaattee() is closed.

       The _m_o_d_e argument (minus any bits set in the process’s uummaasskk(2)) speci‐
       fies the permissions used for creating the new directory in ssppuuffss.  See
       ssttaatt(2) for a full list of the possible _m_o_d_e values.

       The _n_e_i_g_h_b_o_r___f_d is used only when the SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__AAFFFFIINNIITTYY__SSPPUU  flag  is
       specified; see below.

       The  _f_l_a_g_s argument can be zero or any bitwise OR‐ed combination of the
       following constants:

       SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__EEVVEENNTTSS__EENNAABBLLEEDD
              Rather than using signals for  reporting  DMA  errors,  use  the
              _e_v_e_n_t argument to ssppuu__rruunn(2).

       SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__GGAANNGG
              Create  an SPU gang instead of a context.  (A gang is a group of
              SPU contexts that are functionally related  to  each  other  and
              which  share  common  scheduling parameters—priority and policy.
              In the future, gang scheduling may be  implemented  causing  the
              group to be switched in and out as a single unit.)

              A new directory will be created at the location specified by the
              _p_a_t_h  argument.   This  gang  may be used to hold other SPU con‐
              texts, by providing a pathname that is within the gang directory
              to further calls to ssppuu__ccrreeaattee().

       SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__NNOOSSCCHHEEDD
              Create a context that is not  affected  by  the  SPU  scheduler.
              Once  the  context is run, it will not be scheduled out until it
              is destroyed by the creating process.

              Because the context cannot be removed from the SPU,  some  func‐
              tionality  is  disabled for SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__NNOOSSCCHHEEDD contexts.  Only a
              subset of the files will be available in this context  directory
              in ssppuuffss.  Additionally, SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__NNOOSSCCHHEEDD contexts cannot dump
              a core file when crashing.

              Creating  SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__NNOOSSCCHHEEDD  contexts requires the CCAAPP__SSYYSS__NNIICCEE
              capability.

       SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__IISSOOLLAATTEE
              Create an isolated SPU context.  Isolated contexts are protected
              from some PPE (PowerPC Processing Element) operations,  such  as
              access to the SPU local store and the NPC register.

              Creating  SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__IISSOOLLAATTEE contexts also requires the SSPPUU__CCRREE‐‐
              AATTEE__NNOOSSCCHHEEDD flag.

       SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__AAFFFFIINNIITTYY__SSPPUU (since Linux 2.6.23)
              Create a context with affinity to  another  SPU  context.   This
              affinity  information  is  used  within the SPU scheduling algo‐
              rithm.  Using this flag requires that a file  descriptor  refer‐
              ring to the other SPU context be passed in the _n_e_i_g_h_b_o_r___f_d argu‐
              ment.

       SSPPUU__CCRREEAATTEE__AAFFFFIINNIITTYY__MMEEMM (since Linux 2.6.23)
              Create  a context with affinity to system memory.  This affinity
              information is used within the SPU scheduling algorithm.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEE
       On success, ssppuu__ccrreeaattee() returns a new file descriptor.  On failure, -1
       is returned, and _e_r_r_n_o is set to indicate the error.

